(textkit's unit tests - the library gating itself, in this repo, with Vox
 as its only dependency.

 Where textkit_demo.vox shows the library being used, this shows it being
 held to account: many small named claims, each one checked, and a tally
 at the end. A claim that does not hold prints

   ASSERT <name>: expected <x> got <y>

 and the program exits 1, so `make test` fails on it. Nothing else is
 printed but the section headings and the tally, which is why the expected
 transcript is short: silence is the whole point of a passing test.

 A list of text prints the way Vox writes the literal, brackets, quotes,
 and all, which is what lets a list claim be checked as text just like
 everything else.

   vox textkit.vox --shared -o libtextkit.so
   vox textkit_tests.vox --link textkit -o textkit_tests
   ./textkit_tests)

see textkit version "0.2" from "./libtextkit.lib".

a number called 'assertions made' is 0.
a number called failures is 0.

(Every claim goes through here, so a failure always reads the same way and
 the tally at the end is the whole verdict.)
To check with a text called name and a text called wanted and a text called got.
  increment 'assertions made'.
  If got is wanted then, Return.
  increment failures.
  Print "ASSERT {name}: expected {wanted} got {got}".

(The fields 'split on' found, written out the way Vox prints a list of
 text, so the claim can be checked as text like everything else.)
To 'split on for the tests' with a text called content and a text called separator.
  a list called fields is 'split on' of content and separator.
  Return a text, "{fields}".

(The lines 'split lines' found, written out the same way.)
To 'split lines for the tests' with a text called content.
  a list called lines is 'split lines' of content.
  Return a text, "{lines}".

(Splitting content on separator and joining the fields back with the same
 separator, so a round trip claim can compare texts.)
To 'split on and rejoin' with a text called content and a text called separator.
  a list called fields is 'split on' of content and separator.
  Return a text, join of fields and separator.

Print "-- split on --".
check with "fields between plain separators" and "[\"a\", \"b\", \"c\"]" and {'split on for the tests' with "a,b,c" and ","}.
check with "empty fields are kept" and "[\"a\", \"\", \"b\"]" and {'split on for the tests' with "a,,b" and ","}.
check with "a trailing separator yields a trailing empty field" and "[\"a\", \"b\", \"\"]" and {'split on for the tests' with "a,b," and ","}.
check with "a leading separator yields a leading empty field" and "[\"\", \"a\", \"b\"]" and {'split on for the tests' with ",a,b" and ","}.
check with "the empty text splits into one empty field" and "[\"\"]" and {'split on for the tests' with "" and ","}.
check with "an empty separator returns the whole text as one element" and "[\"a,b\"]" and {'split on for the tests' with "a,b" and ""}.
check with "a separator longer than one byte" and "[\"a\", \"b\", \"c\"]" and {'split on for the tests' with "a::b::c" and "::"}.
check with "consecutive separators keep every empty field between them" and "[\"a\", \"\", \"\", \"b\"]" and {'split on for the tests' with "a,,,b" and ","}.

Print "-- split lines --".
check with "one line per newline" and "[\"one\", \"two\", \"three\"]" and {'split lines for the tests' with "one\ntwo\nthree"}.
check with "a trailing newline adds no trailing empty line" and "[\"one\", \"two\"]" and {'split lines for the tests' with "one\ntwo\n"}.
check with "a carriage return right before the newline is dropped" and "[\"one\", \"two\"]" and {'split lines for the tests' with "one\r\ntwo\r\n"}.
check with "a blank line survives between two others" and "[\"one\", \"\", \"two\"]" and {'split lines for the tests' with "one\n\ntwo"}.
check with "the empty text has no lines" and "[]" and {'split lines for the tests' with ""}.
check with "a carriage return not before a newline is not touched" and "[\"a\rb\", \"c\"]" and {'split lines for the tests' with "a\rb\nc"}.

Print "-- replace --".
check with "every occurrence is replaced left to right" and "bXXa" and {replace of "banana" and "an" and "X"}.
check with "matches do not overlap" and "bb" and {replace of "aaaa" and "aa" and "b"}.
check with "a needle that is not there leaves the text unchanged" and "hello" and {replace of "hello" and "z" and "X"}.
check with "an empty needle returns the text unchanged" and "hello" and {replace of "hello" and "" and "X"}.
check with "a needle at the very start" and "Xbc" and {replace of "abc" and "a" and "X"}.
check with "a needle at the very end" and "abX" and {replace of "abc" and "c" and "X"}.

Print "-- round trips --".
check with "join of 'split on' is the original text" and "a,,b," and {'split on and rejoin' with "a,,b," and ","}.
check with "the round trip holds even with an empty separator" and "a,b" and {'split on and rejoin' with "a,b" and ""}.

Print "{'assertions made'} assertions, {failures} failures".
If failures is greater than 0 then, exit 1.
